hadiths[6]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1616","Narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah who said: Waki’ narrated to us. Also narrated to us Salam who said: Abu Mu’awiyah narrated to us. Both of them narrated from al-A’mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from ‘A’ishah (RA) who said:\n\nWhen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill with the illness from which he died, Bilal (RA) came to him to inform him of the prayer. He (ﷺ) said: “Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.”\n\nWe said: “O Messenger of Allah! Abu Bakr is a man who is very emotional. Whenever he stands in your place, he weeps and cannot lead properly. Why not command Umar to lead the people in prayer?”\n\nHe (ﷺ) said: “Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer,” three times. “For indeed you are the companions of Yusuf.”\n\nShe (RA) said: So we sent word to Abu Bakr and he led the people in prayer. The Prophet (ﷺ) then felt some relief and came out, supported by two men, his feet dragging on the ground. When Abu Bakr (RA) sensed him, he tried to step back, but the Prophet (ﷺ) gestured to him to remain in his place. He (ﷺ) then came and sat beside Abu Bakr (RA). Abu Bakr (RA) now followed the prayer of the Prophet (ﷺ), and the people followed the prayer of Abu Bakr (RA). May Allah be pleased with him.”\n\nThis is the narration of Waki’. In the narration of Abu Mu’awiyah, it is stated: “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sitting, while Abu Bakr was standing.”\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: Some people of hadith have said: “If the imam who is ill prays sitting, those behind him should pray standing if they are able.” They said: “The report of al-Aswad and ‘Urwah from ‘A’ishah (RA) abrogates the reports we have mentioned earlier regarding the Prophet’s (ﷺ) command to his companions to sit when the imam prays sitting.”\n\nThey said: “Because those earlier reports were at the time when the Prophet (ﷺ) fell from the horse, whereas this report is from the illness in which he died.”\n\nThey said: “The other action abrogates what preceded of his action and statement.”\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: “As for what I hold — and I ask Allah for protection and guidance — if it were established that the Prophet (ﷺ) was the imam during the illness in which he died, then the matter would be as this group of people of hadith have said. However, this has not been established with us, for the narrators have differed regarding this prayer into three different versions.”\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"1617","Narrated to us Hisham, on the authority of his father, from ‘Aishah (RA), and narrated to us Al-A’mash, from Ibrahim, from Al-Aswad, from ‘Aishah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) was the imam.\n\nIt has also been narrated with this chain from ‘Aishah (RA) that she said:\n\Some people say: Abu Bakr (RA) led the prayer in front of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Others say: The Prophet (ﷺ) led the prayer in front of Abu Bakr (RA).\""\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""""
"1618","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, who said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Al-Aswad, from Aisha (RA)."
"1619","It was narrated from 'Ubaidullah bin 'Abdullah bin 'Utbah and Masruq bin al-Ajda' on the authority of 'Aishah (RA) that she said:\n\When Abu Bakr (RA) led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in the (same) row.\""\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""""
"1620","Bunnadār narrated to us, Bakr bin 'Īsā, the companion of Al-Baṣrī, narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Nu'aym bin Abī Hind, from Abī Wā'il, from Masrūq, from 'Ā'ishah (RA), who said: \Abū Bakr (RA) led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in the row behind him.\"""""""
"1621","Narrated to us Bundār, who said: Narrated to us Badal ibn al-Muhabbar, who said: Narrated to us Shu‘bah, from Mūsā ibn Abī ‘Ā’ishah, from ‘Ubaydullāh ibn ‘Abdullāh, from ‘Ā’ishah (RA):\n\n> Abu Bakr (RA) led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in the row behind him.\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \The report did not reach me that the Prophet (ﷺ) was himself the imam during the illness in which he passed away"","" in the prayer in which he was sitting while Abu Bakr (RA) and the people were standing. This is because in the report of Masrūq and ‘Ubaydullāh ibn ‘Abdullāh"","" from ‘Ā’ishah (RA)"","" it is stated that Abu Bakr (RA) was the imam and the Prophet (ﷺ) was the follower. This contradicts the report of Hishām"","" from his father"","" from ‘Ā’ishah (RA)"","" and the report of Ibrāhīm"","" from al-Aswad"","" from ‘Ā’ishah (RA).\n\nHowever"","" Shu‘bah ibn al-Hajjāj clarified in his narration from al-A‘mash"","" from Ibrāhīm"","" from al-Aswad"","" from ‘Ā’ishah (RA)"","" that some people say: Abu Bakr (RA) stood in front of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"","" while others say: The Prophet (ﷺ) stood in front of Abu Bakr (RA).\n\nIf the hadith which those who claim that the act of the Prophet (ﷺ) in his fall from the horse and his command to follow the imams and their sitting in prayer when their imam is sitting is abrogated and not authentic from the perspective of transmission is weak"","" then it is not permissible for a scholar to claim the abrogation of what has been authentically established from the Prophet (ﷺ) by mutawātir reports with sound chains regarding his action and command"","" based on a report that is disputed.\n\nMoreover"","" the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade the act which this group claimed in the hadith of ‘Ā’ishah (RA) that we mentioned—though it is disputed regarding her—saying that it is an act of the Persians and Romans"","" where their leaders stand while their kings sit. We have already mentioned this hadith in its proper place. How then can it be permissible to command what has been authentically established from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the prohibition of following the Persians and Romans"","" without an authentic report from him (ﷺ) commanding and permitting it after the prohibition?\n\nThere is no disagreement among the people of knowledge of hadith that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed sitting"","" and he commanded the people to sit when they were able to stand. If the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded the followers to follow the imam and sit when the imam prays sitting"","" and forbade standing in prayer when the imam prays sitting"","" though they were able to stand"","" and they differed regarding the abrogation of that"","" no report has been established from the perspective of transmission to abrogate what has been authentically established from him (ﷺ) regarding his action and command.\n\nWhat has been authentically established from the Prophet (ﷺ) and agreed upon by the people of knowledge is certain"","" while what they differed over and for which no authentic report has been established is doubtful. It is not permissible to abandon certainty for doubt; rather"","" certainty can only be abandoned for another certainty.\n\nIf someone says—without thorough reflection—'How can it be permissible for one who is able to stand to pray sitting?' It is said to him: 'If Allah wills"","" it is permissible for him to pray sitting"","" and that is more befitting. It is the Sunnah of the Prophet (ﷺ)"","" whose obedience he commanded"","" and he promised guidance for following it. He (ﷺ) informed that obedience to him is obedience to Allah"","" the Exalted. Saying 'How can it be permissible?' when it has been authentically established from the Prophet (ﷺ) by mutawātir reports with sound chains is ignorance on the part of the one who says it.\n\nIt has been authentically established from the Prophet (ﷺ)"","" according to all the people of knowledge of hadith"","" that he commanded praying sitting when the imam prays sitting"","" and it is also established that he (ﷺ) prayed sitting while his companions were sitting"","" without any illness on their part or on his.\n\nA group claimed the abrogation of that"","" but their claim has not been established by any authentic hadith without contradiction. Therefore"","" it is not permissible to abandon what has been authentically established from his (ﷺ) command and action at any time"","" except by an authentic hadith from him (ﷺ) that abrogates that command and action.\n\nSince the existence of such an abrogating authentic hadith is non-existent"","" the claim of abrogation is invalid. Hence"","" it is permissible to pray sitting when the imam prays sitting"","" following the command and action of the Prophet (ﷺ). And Allah is the One who grants success to what is correct.\""\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""""
